{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 43, :comments ["<b>Rejoice, O nations, with His people.</b> For the nations will praise Israel and burst forth in song over them, because they held fast to their good faith and did not turn aside from the way during the time of exile. Or the sense of הרנינו may be from the language of proclamation — that the nations will proclaim concerning them in every place where they are, in the manner of (I Kings 22) ״ויעבור הרנה״." "<b>For the blood of His servants He will avenge.</b> Moses prophesied here concerning the troubles and events destined to pass over us in our exile — both through the shedding of the blood of Israel, when the nations would kill them, and through the loss of their property by robbery and violence that would be done to them; and in the manner of what is written concerning the seed of Esau (Obadiah): ״מחמס אחיך יעקב תכסך בושה ונכרת לעולםם״; and concerning this he said, ״For the blood of His servants He will avenge, and vengeance He will return to His adversaries.״" "<b>And He will atone His land, His people.</b> The sense is: His land and His people. And in the Midrash: ״And He will atone His land upon His people.״ And at the end of Ketubot, our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: ״וכפר אדמתו עמו״ — all who are buried in the land of Israel are as if buried beneath the altar; it is written here ״וכפר אדמתו עמו״, and it is written there (Exodus 20:21) ״מזבח אדמה תעשה לי״. And this was part of the mystery of the formation of man, that the Holy One, blessed be He, created him from the place of his atonement, as it is written of him (Genesis 2:7) ״עפר מן האדמה״, and this is the excellence of the land, that it is the palace of the King and is called the land of YHWH and the inheritance of YHWH, and all that is outside it is called in the language of Scripture ״the outgoings״ (תוצאות) and ״the tents of wickedness״ (אהלי רשע), and in the language of our Rabbis of blessed memory ״the land of the nations״ (ארץ העמים) — and behold, it is impure and imparts impurity by virtue of its dust and its air. And so they said in the first chapter of Gittin: ״The land of the nations — they decreed impurity upon its clods and upon its air; but Syria — upon its clods they decreed, upon its air they did not decree.״ And I have already mentioned of this in the verse (Deuteronomy 31:16) ״אלהי נכר הארץם״." "<b>And He will atone His land, His people.</b> That is, that the land will be purified from the corpses of the enemies; and the language of וכפר is like וטהר, as it is written (Ezekiel 43) ״וחטאת אותו וכפרתהו״, and it is written in Ezekiel (ibid. 39) ״וטהרו הארץ״; and he said this concerning Israel — that they will purify the land at the end of the wars. <br>And it says <b>and there is no god with Me</b> — that is, I am one and there is no other with Me; and he said this in response to what the enemies said, ״let them arise and help you״ — in plural language; therefore it was necessary to say that He, blessed be He, is one and there is no other with Him. And from what he said, ״I put to death and I bring to life״ — I am He who puts Israel to death and I bring them to life — from here our Rabbis of blessed memory derived the matter of the revival of the dead; and so they said: just as wounding and healing are in one, so too death and life are in one; and it is in the manner of what is written (I Samuel 2) ״YHWH puts to death and brings to life, brings down to Sheol and raises up.״ And the intent of our Rabbis of blessed memory when they said ״just as wounding and healing are in one״ is that since Scripture did not say אמחץ ואני ארפא — which would have implied ״I wound Reuben and I heal Simeon״ — but rather changed and said מחצתי, He means to say concerning that very same person: I who put to death — I will revive him." "<b>And know</b> that many will wonder and ask: why was the matter of the revival of the dead not mentioned explicitly in the Torah, seeing that it is a great principle and a major pillar in the Torah? How many passages of the Torah does Scripture lengthen and repeat in which there is not so great a need; if so, why would Scripture be brief in this matter and deliver a great principle by means of a slight hint? And the answer to this is the matter that I have written to you several times: that the way of the Torah is to be brief concerning hidden matters, and whatever is more hidden, Scripture is briefer concerning it, and speech concerning it is only by hint and in brevity. For so you find in the account of Creation, that it was brief concerning the matter of the first light and lengthened on the third day concerning the creation of trees and plants. And so you find in David, peace be upon him, that he grasped this approach and spoke of the light in brevity — as he said (Psalms 104:2) ״עוטה אור כשלמה״. And he was brief also concerning the upper waters and lengthened concerning the lower waters: brief concerning the upper waters, as he said (ibid.) ״המקרה במים עליותיו״ — for עליותיו, which are the worlds He created, He covered them within the waters; and lengthened concerning the lower waters, as he said (ibid.) ״תהום כלבוש כסיתו על הרים יעמדו מים מן גערתך ינוסון וגו׳ יעלו הרים וגו׳ גבול שמת בל יעבורון וגו׳ המשלח מעינים בנחלים וגו׳״. This and yet another matter: all the promises in the Torah that are stated through Moses are only bodily promises that human beings can testify to within a short time, in order to establish in the heart the truth of the prophecy of Moses; and through those bodily promises the hidden intellectual matters will be verified for them; therefore Scripture was brief concerning them on account of their concealed nature — this is the reason for the concealment of the matter of the revival of the dead. Also the matter of the World to Come, which is not mentioned explicitly in the Torah either — this too is due to its concealment, for the Torah was given to the multitude, and the understanding of the multitude cannot grasp the intellectual matters; and so the Torah delivers them to a small and subtle hint, which the multitude will pass over and the discerning individual will be roused to it. And if you consider the word סתר״ה רא״ו עת״ה כ״י, you will find in it the special Name inscribed in reverse, and it comes to allude to the attribute of judgment stretched over the nations at the time of redemption; and this is what he mentioned immediately: ״ותאחז במשפט ידי אשיב נקם לצרי״, and he said: ״אשכיר חצי מדם וחרבי וגו׳״." "<b>And you will find</b> in the Torah, from Genesis until לעיני כל ישראל, eighteen instances of the Name whether in sequential order at the beginnings of words and at their ends, or in reverse at the beginnings of words and at their ends. And since this one is the last of them all, I have seen fit to arrange all of them here and to alert you concerning them. For the eight that are in sequential order are four at the beginnings of words and four at the ends of words, and these are: (Genesis 2:1) <b>י</b>ום <b>ה</b>ששי <b>ו</b>יכלו <b>ה</b>שמים, (Genesis 38:24) <b>י</b>הודה <b>ה</b>וציאוה <b>ו</b>תשרף <b>ה</b>יא, (Exodus 4:14) <b>י</b>דבר <b>ה</b>וא <b>ו</b>גם <b>ה</b>נה, (Deuteronomy 11:2–3) <b>י</b>דו <b>ה</b>חזקה <b>ו</b>זרועו <b>ה</b>נטויה, (Genesis 19:13) את פנ<b>י</b> יהו<b>ה</b> וישלחנ<b>ו</b> יהו<b>ה</b>, (Exodus 3:13) ל<b>י</b> מ<b>ה</b> שמ<b>ו</b> מ<b>ה</b>, (Deuteronomy 24:5) נק<b>י</b> יהי<b>ה</b> לבית<b>ו</b> שנ<b>ה</b>, (Deuteronomy 30:12) מ<b>י</b> יעל<b>ה</b> לנ<b>ו</b> השמימ<b>ה</b>. And the ten are in reverse, and in every place where the unique Name appears in reverse it is the attribute of justice, and these are: (Genesis 11:9) <b>ה</b>ארץ <b>ו</b>משם <b>ה</b>פיצם <b>י</b>הוה — for the attribute of justice was stretched over them because they sinned in cutting down the plantings; and likewise (Genesis 44:10) <b>ה</b> מה <b>ו</b>חמוריהם <b>ה</b>ם <b>י</b>צאו — for it was stretched over them in the sin of recognition; and likewise (Exodus 4:16) <b>ה</b>עם <b>ו</b>היה <b>ה</b>וא <b>י</b>היה — for it was stretched over Moses on account of his refusal; and thus it is written (Exodus 4:14) ויחר אף ה׳ במשה; and likewise (Exodus 4:3) ארצ<b>ה</b> וישליכה<b>ו</b> ארצ<b>ה</b> ויה<b>י</b> — for the sign of the staff that turned into a serpent was by the reversal of the letters of the great Name; and likewise (Exodus 16:7) יהו<b>ה</b> ונחנ<b>ו</b> מ<b>ה</b> כ<b>י</b> — for it was stretched against them on account of the demand for meat; and likewise Scripture began with the attribute of justice (Exodus 16:7) ובקר וראיתם את כבוד ה׳; and likewise (Exodus 26:22) <b>ה</b> אחד <b>ו</b>לירכתי <b>ה</b>משכן <b>י</b>מה — for the Shekhinah, which is the attribute of justice, was in the west; and likewise (Numbers 1:51) <b>ה</b>לוים <b>ו</b>בחנות <b>ה</b>משכן <b>י</b>קימו, (Numbers 1:51) <b>ה</b>לוים <b>ו</b>הזר <b>ה</b>קרב <b>י</b>ומת — it was stretched against them because the Ark would consume them; and likewise (Numbers 5:18) <b>ה</b>יא <b>ו</b>ביד <b>ה</b>כהן <b>י</b>היו — it was stretched against the sotah if she had defiled herself; and likewise (Numbers 14:25) שמ<b>ה</b> וזרע<b>ו</b> יורישנ<b>ה</b> והעמלק<b>י</b> — it was stretched against Israel in the sin of the spies, and they were forced to turn back by way of the Sea of Reeds; and that is what it says (Numbers 14:43) כי על כן שבתם מאחרי ה׳; and likewise here (Deuteronomy 32:43) סתר<b>ה</b> רא<b>ו</b> עת<b>ה</b> כ<b>י</b> — because in the future the attribute of justice will be stretched against the nations, and that is why it immediately mentions אשכיר חצי מדם. The eight that are in sequential order contain an allusion to the attribute of mercy, and the ten that are in reverse all indicate the attribute of justice; and so they are eighteen in all, and their sign is ח״י ה׳ — and this is clear."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {4 "The final sentence's citation 'אשכיר חצי מדם וחרבי' does not correspond to any verse in this parashah; it appears textually corrupt — the immediate context is Deuteronomy 32:41 'אשנן ברק חצי וגו׳', and the printed text is retained hyper-literally. The acrostic סתר״ה רא״ו עת״ה כ״י is obscure; transcribed exactly as in the source.", 5 "Citation (Genesis 44:10) appears as ש״ם מ״ד in the source; resolved to Genesis 44:10 based on the quoted text. Several other numbered citations similarly normalized from the abbreviated forms as written. The reference לעיני כל ישראל points to the end of the Torah (Deuteronomy 34:12)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:54:17.618461117Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}